ed at the last three Olympics. At the World Championships, the change from a crew of five to four had first occurred in 1985 and, since then, the East Germans had won every time. In Seoul the country was represented by " [:a {:href "/athletes/37532"} "Kerstin Förster"] ", " [:a {:href "/athletes/37708"} "Kristina Mundt"] ", " [:a {:href "/athletes/37728"} "Beate Schramm"] ", and " [:a {:href "/athletes/37564"} "Jana Sorgers"] ", all of whom except for Schramm had World Championship titles. No other nation had been able to challenge the East Germans consistently, although the Romanian quartet included " [:a {:href "/athletes/39490"} "Veronica Cogeanu"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/39517"} "Elisabeta Lipă"] ", who were beginning their journey of becoming the most decorated rowers in Olympic history. In the opening round, however, it was Bulgaria, the most recent World runner-up, who led the field, winning its heat and defeating the Romanians in the process. Its crew included " [:a {:href "/athletes/35942"} "Iskra Velinova"] ", a 1980 Olympic silver medalist in the coxed fours. East Germany captured the other heat, albeit in a much slower time, but rebounded in the final and took the gold medal by a comfortable margin. The Soviet crew took silver and included " [:a {:href "/athletes/40157"} "Antonina Dumcheva"] ", the runner-up in the 1980 Olympic single sculls. Bulgaria, meanwhile, faltered in the medal around and allowed Romania to sneak ahead of it and claim bronze."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Competitors"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {} "East Germany"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/GDR"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/GDR.png"}] "GDR"]] [:td {} [:span {:class "Gold"} "Gold"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {}] [:td {:colsp